  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nLooking at satellite across the area this morning, low stratus\nfrom this morning is eroding from the east with clear skies over\nOmaha, broken skies at Lincoln, and cloudy conditions at\nColumbus and Norfolk. A trough moving into the Inter-Mountain\nWest is amplifying winds over the North and Central Plains,\nleading to southerly winds gusting 30 to 40 mph across our area\nthis afternoon into the evening. Strong winds continue into \nWednesday as this system moves across the Dakotas into\nMinnesota. Drier air pushes into our northwestern counties\nWednesday afternoon behind a dryline that lowers humidity to\n25-30 percent over areas west of a line from Wayne to Columbus.\nBy the time this drier air arrives winds should be much weaker,\nreducing fire weather concerns. Clearing skies on Wednesday \nshould allow for temperatures to warm into the mid-60s across \nour area.\n\nThe upper-level pattern remains active through late in the week\nwith a transient ridge moving through Thursday keeping skies\nclear and winds relatively calm. The next West-Coast trough\nmoving in will again ramp up southerly winds on Friday with\ngusts again reaching 30-40 mph, potentially up to 45 mph Friday\nafternoon. Rain chances return as a cold front moves through\nFriday night into Saturday. With the base of the trough \ntrekking to our south across Oklahoma and SE Kansas into SW \nMissouri, any chance for stronger storms should stay well \nsoutheast of our area. Strong frontogenetical forcing across our\nfar southeastern counties, though, could lead to some storms \nwith enhanced rainfall rates Saturday evening. \n\nRain clears out by Sunday morning as the front exits with cooler\nair moving in behind it. After several days with highs in the\n60s Wed-Fri, we'll see temperatures drop into the 20s Sunday\nmorning, only warming into the 40s Sunday afternoon. Ensemble\nguidance are all in fairly good agreement that the cooler air\nwill remain in place through Thanksgiving week. The GEFS \nholds temperatures a bit cooler than the ECMWF Ensemble. Both \nensembles indicate a potential system Tuesday-Wednesday next \nweek, with varying degrees of cool air in place with a chance \nsomewhere sees the first snow flakes of the year across our \narea. For now confidence remains low, but with Thanksgiving \ntravel picking up around that time, we'll want to monitor this \npotential system closely.",
